    "chunk_size_bytes": 1922,
    "chunk_text": "Businessman files bribery complaint over alleged Rs. 3.2 mn Ports Authority fraud\n\nColombo, June 1 (Daily Mirror) - A businessman from Boralesgamuwa has lodged a complaint with the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption seeking an investigation into an alleged fraud involving more than Rs. 3.2 million connected to officials of the Sri Lanka Ports Authority.\n\nAccording to the complaint, the businessman alleged that he was misled into believing that a tender for the supply of goods had been awarded to the Ports Authority and subsequently paid large sums of money to several individuals connected to the institution.\n\nThe complainant, identified as Damseth Dineth Amaratunga, claimed that the alleged fraud had taken place within the office of the Managing Director of the Ports Authority on Darley Road, Colombo, with the involvement of an officer attached to that office.\n\nHe further alleged that the Managing Director of the Ports Authority had refused to meet him when he attempted to raise concerns regarding the incident.\n\nAccording to the complaint, Amaratunga said that an officer attached to the Managing Director\u2019s office had introduced him to the Managing Director\u2019s secretary and that he had handed over Rs. 600,000 on three separate occasions at the office premises.\n\nHe also claimed that he transferred Rs. 1,697,600 to the personal bank account of a female Ports Authority officer on six occasions and paid a further Rs. 368,000 to the Ports Finance Division.\n\nThe complaint further said that attempts to seek redress from port officials had been unsuccessful as the individuals involved allegedly avoided him.\n\nAmaratunga also claimed that, through the intervention of another senior port official, the matter was brought to the attention of Deputy Minister of Ports Janitha Kodituwakku, who had instructed him to lodge a complaint with the Criminal Investigation Department."
  },
